# OWASP AI Security & Privacy

Lifecycle, governance, and privacy controls. Prompt injection, output handling, agency, and consumption limits are in the `owasp-llm-top10` rule — do not duplicate them here.

## Data governance
- Classify data entering and leaving AI components (PII, PHI, credentials, confidential)
- Document data sources, legal basis/consent, and allowed purposes; prefer references over raw payloads
- Minimise retention of prompts, completions, and intermediates; justify and time-box any storage

## Privacy by design
- Support data-subject rights: access, deletion, and opt-out — including from logs, caches, vector stores, and fine-tuning sets
- Pseudonymise or aggregate data used for evaluation and analytics
- Assess privacy impact for new AI features that process personal data (e.g. DPIA where regulation requires)
- Be transparent with users when they interact with AI or when AI makes decisions about them

## Model lifecycle
- Version prompts, tool definitions, safety policies, and model configuration as code
- Evaluate model or prompt changes in staging with regression and safety eval suites before rollout
- Document intended use, known limitations, and evaluation results (model/system cards)
- Keep an inventory (AI-BOM) of models, providers, and datasets in use

## Monitoring and incident response
- Capture operational telemetry (latency, tokens, cost, error and refusal rates) without sensitive content
- Monitor for drift, abuse patterns, and quality regressions
- Maintain runbooks for data leakage, poisoning, abuse, and provider outage; have a kill switch for AI features

## Auditability and compliance
- Record rationale and inputs (by reference) for automated decisions that affect users
- Keep human oversight for high-impact decisions and a path for users to contest them
- Track applicable regulation (e.g. EU AI Act risk classification, sector rules)

## Reviewer checklist
- [ ] Data classified; sources and purposes documented
- [ ] Retention minimised; deletion covers logs, caches, vector stores
- [ ] Prompts, tools, and policies versioned and evaluated before rollout
- [ ] Telemetry free of sensitive content; drift/abuse/cost alerts exist
- [ ] Automated decisions auditable with human oversight where impactful
